locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S2 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 has "labour_his"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3 have "own_person → labour_his"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S2: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1, to: Suppose "every man has a property in his own person, which nobody but himself has any right to" and "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his" are false. Is S2, "The labour of his body, and the work of his hands, we may say, are properly his.", true then? [in 1 of 3 runs]
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 has "removes → mixed"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3 have "removes → mixed" and "excludes → property"; they differ when only "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" and "something is annexed to the thing that excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" are true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1.2 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own" and "something is annexed to the thing that excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" are true and "the thing is made his property" is false. Is S3, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then?
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.2 have "mixed ∧ labour_his ∧ enough_left → excludes"; locke-labour.logic.r1.3 has "removes" and "labour_his ∧ mixed ∧ enough_left → excludes"; they differ when only "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own", "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "something is annexed to the thing that excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" are true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his", "he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own", "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "something is annexed to the thing that excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" are true and "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" is false. Is S4, "It being by him removed from the common state nature hath placed it in, it hath by this labour something annexed to it, that excludes the common right of other men: for this labour being the unquestionable property of the labourer, no man but he can have a right to what that is once joined to, at least where there is enough, and as good, left in common for others.", true then? [in 2 of 3 runs]
locke-labour.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 has "excludes → property"; locke-labour.logic.r1.2 and locke-labour.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "something is annexed to the thing that excludes the common right of other men, so that no man but he can have a right to it" is true, which the second allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: locke-labour.logic.r1 and locke-labour.logic.r1.2 have "removes ∧ enough_left → property"; locke-labour.logic.r1.3 has "property"; they differ when only "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" is true, which the first allows
locke-labour.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps locke-labour.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in" is true and "there is enough, and as good, left in common for others" and "the thing is made his property" are false. Is the conclusion, "Whatsoever then he removes out of the state that nature hath provided, and left it in, he hath mixed his labour with, and joined to it something that is his own, and thereby makes it his property.", true then? [in 1 of 3 runs]
